  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Lilac Fibrecap is a small woodland mushroom characterized by its distinctive lilac to purple-tinged cap and fine, fibrous texture. This species belongs to the Inocybe family, known for their delicate appearance and thin, fragile stems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Lilac Cap Color",
      "description": "The cap displays a subtle lilac, purple, or lavender hue, especially visible on young specimens and often fading with age or moisture loss",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Fibrous Texture",
      "description": "The cap surface shows fine, radial fibers that give it a silky or streaked appearance rather than being smooth",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Crowded Brown Gills",
      "description": "Gills are closely spaced and brown to rust-colored, with a distinctive angular or notched attachment to the stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in hardwood and mixed forests throughout New England, typically fruiting in spring through fall among leaf litter and mossy areas.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like many Inocybe species, Lilac Fibrecap contains trace amounts of psilocybin, making it toxic and potentially psychoactive, though it is rarely encountered in significant quantities.",
